.\" karvi-run(1): the manual page of karvi's run word. Written by hand except
.\" the regions between the markers, which tools/mangen rewrites from the
.\" word's help text (make generate).
.TH KARVI-RUN 1 "" "karvi"
.\" Paths and options are not hyphenated, and lines are not stretched.
.nh
.ds AD l
.ad l
.SH NAME
karvi\-run \- run commands against an explicit scope
.SH SYNOPSIS
.\" BEGIN GENERATED SYNOPSIS: tools/mangen from internal/cli; edit there
\fBkarvi\fR \fBrun\fR [target inputs] [options] <device command words...>
.br
\fBkarvi\fR \fBrun\fR [target inputs] [options] \-\-cmd TEXT [\-\-cmd TEXT...]
.br
\fBkarvi\fR \fBrun\fR [target inputs] [options] \-\-cf PATH|\-
.\" END GENERATED SYNOPSIS
.SH DESCRIPTION
.\" BEGIN GENERATED DESCRIPTION: tools/mangen from internal/cli; edit there
.SS "Target inputs (at least one; command\-line order is kept, the first occurrence of a name keeps its position, names are lowercase)"
.TP
\fB\-\-target\fR \fINAME\fR, \fB\-\-host\fR, \fB\-\-t\fR
Repeatable inventory or direct target, or glob
.TP
\fB\-\-tl\fR \fILIST\fR
Targets in one argument, separated by commas
or whitespace (quote a whitespace list); each
is a \-\-target at this position
.TP
\fB\-\-tf\fR \fIFILE|PATH|\-\fR
Target file, or the files directly in a
folder; \- reads stdin
.TP
\fB\-\-tfr\fR \fIPATH\fR
Target file, or a folder and its subfolders
(targets.recursion\-max\-depth, default 3)
.TP
\fB\-\-site\fR \fIGLOB\fR, \fB\-\-device\-group\fR \fIGLOB\fR, \fB\-\-select\-platform\fR \fIGLOB\fR, \fB\-\-all\fR
Inventory selectors, matches at this position
.TP
\fB\-\-exclude\fR \fIGLOB\fR
Remove name matches after duplicate removal
.PP
A GLOB matches the whole value without regard to case: * ? [a\-c] [!a\-c],
and \e escapes the next character; ^ and $ are refused. A selector value
beginning with ! removes the devices it matches and selects none; in a
target file, lines beginning with # or ! are comments.
A \-\-select\-platform value is a selector over the known platforms (the
built\-ins and the configured [platform.NAME] tables) and matches the
platform set on a device's row or source default, before any \-\-platform
acts; a value reaching no known platform is refused. A device whose platform
is not set, or falls back under platform\-resolution.on\-unknown = "warn", is
matched by no \-\-select\-platform value (it stays reachable by name, site,
group, and \-\-all).
An unknown row platform in the set refuses the run under the default, fail.
.TP
\fB\-\-order\fR \fIdefault|sorted|shuffle|random\fR
Dispatch order for this invocation (sets the
lock\-aware dispatch.order; the shuffle key
comes from dispatch.shuffle\-key)
.PP
Folder reads skip names beginning with . readme or disabled, and editor
backups (~ .bak .swp .orig .rej #name#); an empty source follows
targets.empty\-source (error or warn).
.SS "Device commands (one form per invocation)"
.PP
Positional words are joined with one space into one command.
.TP
\fB\-\-cmd\fR \fITEXT\fR, \fB\-\-command\fR, \fB\-\-c\fR
Separate command; repeatable
.TP
\fB\-\-cf\fR \fIPATH|\-\fR
One command per line, sent as written; at most once
.PP
A command ending in \er sequences is sent without them, followed by that
many carriage returns before the prompt is read (a [confirm] answer), and is
blind; nothing else in the text is interpreted.
.SS "Interactive prompts (each attaches to the \-\-cmd it follows, or to the one freeform command; a command takes blind returns or \-\-expect, not both)"
.TP
\fB\-\-expect\fR \fIPATTERN=RESPONSE\fR
When a line matching PATTERN (RE2) appears,
type RESPONSE and a return; consumed once, in
declared order; empty RESPONSE is a bare
return; at most 20 per command. A response is
device text, recorded in clear: not for a
password
.TP
\fB\-\-blind\fR
The prompt may not return: await the blind
wait in place of the command timeout, and a
prompt never seen is still a success with the
notice prompt_not_observed_after_blind_send
.TP
\fB\-\-blind\-return\fR \fIN\fR
Send N carriage returns with the command
(0..20), before any read, and treat it as
\-\-blind
.TP
\fB\-\-blind\-wait\fR \fIDURATION\fR
Prompt\-return wait for a blind command, in
place of the command timeout (0 does not wait;
sets the lock\-aware execution.blind\-wait)
.TP
\fB\-\-literal\fR
Send every command as written; no \er
interpretation; a RESPONSE is always as written
.SS "A command's own bounds (each attaches to the \-\-cmd it follows, or to the one freeform command; at most one of each per command)"
.TP
\fB\-\-timeout\fR \fIDURATION\fR
The command's timeout in place of
execution.command\-timeout, and over telnet of
telnet.read\-timeout (1s..12h); not on a blind
command, nor above a set
execution.device\-timeout
.TP
\fB\-\-maxbytes\fR \fIBYTES\fR
The command's output limit in place of
output.max\-command\-bytes, in whole bytes
(1024..1073741824); not above
output.max\-job\-bytes
.SS "Platform"
.TP
\fB\-\-platform\fR \fINAME\fR
The platform the devices of this invocation run
as, whatever their inventory rows say: a
built\-in or a configured [platform.NAME]
table; a name, never a pattern. login and
command apply it to their one device, run to
every device of the set (a direct target in no
inventory runs as generic without it).
Without it an inventory row's platform
applies, then the source default, then
platform\-resolution.default (else generic); an
unknown row platform is refused unless
platform\-resolution.on\-unknown = "warn"
.TP
\fB\-\-pi\fR, \fB\-\-pn\fR, \fB\-\-pr\fR, \fB\-\-pj\fR, \fB\-\-pa\fR, \fB\-\-pg\fR
Short for \-\-platform cisco_iosxe, cisco_nxos,
cisco_iosxr, juniper_junos, arista_eos, generic
.SS "Dispatch"
.TP
\fB\-\-dispatch\fR \fIserial|parallel|wave\fR
How the devices run (dispatch.default, serial
by default): one at a time; a fixed pool of
workers over one queue; or waves whose width
follows the host's CPU
.TP
\fB\-\-dp\fR, \fB\-\-dw\fR, \fB\-\-ds\fR
Short for \-\-dispatch parallel, wave, serial
.TP
\fB\-\-workers\fR \fIN\fR
The parallel pool's size
(dispatch.parallel\-workers; 0 the host's
logical CPUs)
.TP
\fB\-\-start\-width\fR \fIN\fR
A wave job's first width, and its floor
(dispatch.wave\-start\-width; 0 from the host's
CPUs)
.TP
\fB\-\-max\-width\fR \fIN\fR
A wave job's widest wave
(dispatch.wave\-max\-width; 0 from the host's
CPUs)
.TP
\fB\-\-halt\-on\-error\-count\fR \fIN\fR
Start no further device once N have failed;
those in flight finish
(dispatch.halt\-on\-error\-count; 0 off)
.TP
\fB\-\-halt\-on\-error\-percent\fR \fIN\fR
Start no further device once the failed are
N percent or more of the devices ended so
far, checked as each ends, so a first
device's failure halts at any N
(dispatch.halt\-on\-error\-percent; 0 off)
.TP
\fB\-\-wave\-gate\-error\-count\fR \fIN\fR
Between waves, stop a wave job whose last
wave had N failed devices
(dispatch.wave\-gate\-error\-count; 0 off)
.TP
\fB\-\-wave\-gate\-error\-percent\fR \fIN\fR
The same at N percent of that wave's devices
(dispatch.wave\-gate\-error\-percent; 0 off)
.TP
\fB\-\-wave\-delay\fR \fIDURATION\fR
A pause between waves
(dispatch.wave\-gate\-timed\-delay; 0s none)
.TP
\fB\-\-continue\-device\-on\-error\fR
Do not stop later commands after a device error
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-daemon\fR
Execute in the client process
.TP
\fB\-\-nof\fR
No output files: run and display, create no job
folder (output.persist\-command=false); not with
\-\-detach or \-\-exercise
.TP
\fB\-\-of[=PATH]\fR
Output files on (output.persist\-command=true),
to the configured folder or to PATH
(output.root), on every path; PATH only with =
(a path as the next word is refused)
.TP
\fB\-\-cd=PATH\fR
Also write each device's output to PATH/NAME,
one file per device named by the device, a
"! COMMAND" marker before each command's
output and nothing else, replaced only when
the device succeeds (crun.directory; ~
expanded, relative to the working directory;
auto the collection tree)
.TP
\fB\-\-fs=SUFFIX\fR
Append SUFFIX to each collection file's name
(\-\-fs=.cfg writes NAME.cfg); without \-\-cd it
is \-\-cd=. as well
.TP
\fB\-\-follow\fR
Render durable records after completion (default)
.SS "Options"
.TP
\fB\-\-transport\fR \fISELECTOR\fR
default, system, native, preferred, telnet,
or a configured [ssh.transports] slot
.TP
\fB\-\-management\-address\fR \fIIP\fR, \fB\-\-address\fR, \fB\-\-a\fR
Override the address for exactly one selected device
.TP
\fB\-\-address\-authority\fR \fITARGET=client|daemon\fR
Who selects TARGET's address: the client at
planning (default) or the daemon at prepare;
repeatable, TARGET names a device of the set
.TP
\fB\-\-ssh\-host\-key\-policy\fR \fIaccept\-new|secure|insecure\fR
The host\-key policy (ssh.host\-key\-policy):
accept\-new accepts and persists a new key and
rejects a changed one (the default); secure
requires a matching pre\-enrolled key before
access; insecure accepts unknown or changed
keys with prominent warnings
.TP
\fB\-\-ssh\-known\-hosts\-file\fR \fIPATH|auto\fR
Unified karvi trust store
.TP
\fB\-\-format\fR \fItext|jsonl|json\fR
Text (default), compact JSON Lines, or an
indented JSON array for human inspection
.TP
\fB\-\-echo\fR
Show prompt plus sent command in text output
.TP
\fB\-\-border\fR
Replace the configured run border with dashes
.TP
\fB\-\-noborder\fR
Disable configured and dynamic run borders
.TP
\fB\-\-ipv4\fR, \fB\-\-4\fR
Prefer IPv4 when both families are available
.TP
\fB\-\-ipv6\fR, \fB\-\-6\fR
Prefer IPv6 when both families are available
.TP
\fB\-\-detach\fR
Submit and print the job ID and artifact directory;
do not follow; exit 0 means accepted
.TP
\fB\-\-follow\fR, \fB\-\-follow=false\fR
Render records as they become durable (default);
false waits for the result without rendering
.TP
\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR
Plan on the client and probe the daemon; submit
no job, send no target data, contact no device;
print the inspection report in the \-\-format
.TP
\fB\-\-exercise\fR
Submit the plan and package for daemon\-side
validation of everything but the device; no
ICMP, session, askpass, or command; print the
exercise report in the \-\-format
.TP
\fB\-\-ping\fR
Send two ICMP probes to each target before its
transport; skip a target that answers neither
.TP
\fB\-\-noping\fR
Send no ICMP probes (the default unless
network.ping\-targets is true)
.TP
\fB\-\-debug\fR
Safe transport, resolution, and timing diagnostics
.TP
\fB\-\-help\fR, \fB\-\-h\fR
Show this help
.PP
Options are recognized until the first device command word; after it every
argument is device text. Run uses the per\-user daemon unless \-\-no\-daemon is
supplied.
.PP
The preferred native slot maps to scrapligo\-v1 by default; status: available (scrapligo 1.4.2 compiled in).
Builds without that adapter reject native transport rather than silently using OpenSSH.
.PP
Set ssh.halt\-run\-on\-host\-key\-mismatch=true to stop new scheduling after the
first changed host key while allowing already in\-flight devices to finish.
.\" END GENERATED DESCRIPTION
.SH DISPATCH
.B serial
runs one device at a time
.RB ( dispatch.default ).
.B parallel
starts a fixed pool of
.B \-\-workers
over one queue in the dispatch order: a worker that finishes a device
takes the next at once, and the pool never changes size.
.B wave
runs the devices in waves of the width times
.B dispatch.wave\-depth\-multiplier
(4), each with that many workers; a wave ends when its last device ends,
and before the next one karvi applies the error gate, then the delay, then
sizes the next width from the host's CPU (the busy share of every CPU,
smoothed): under the band around
.B dispatch.wave\-cpu\-threshold\-percent
(75, the band 65 to 85) the width grows by half, never past the ceiling;
over it the width shrinks by a tenth, never under the start width, and the
next two decisions keep it.
.PP
At 0 the widths follow the number of the host's logical CPUs,
.IR CPUs :
the pool has
.I CPUs
workers, the start width is
.RI min(64,\ max(16,\ 4\(mu CPUs )),
and the ceiling
.RI min(256,\ max(32,\ 8\(mu CPUs )).
With 4 logical CPUs a wave job starts at 16 devices and widens to at most
32; with 8, from 32 to 64; with 32 or more, from 64 to 256.
The ceiling's formula is also the host's cap,
.B dispatch.server\-max\-inflight
at 0 (32, 64, and 256 on those hosts): the device sessions in flight at
once across every job and every operator on the host, held as leases.
One wave job at its ceiling can fill the cap alone; beside other jobs a
worker past the cap waits for a lease before it connects, so a pool or a
ceiling above the cap adds workers and no sessions.
.PP
The halts apply in every mode:
.B \-\-halt\-on\-error\-count
.I N
starts no further device once
.I N
have failed, and
.B \-\-halt\-on\-error\-percent
.I N
once the failed are
.I N
percent or more of the devices ended so far, checked as each ends, so a
first device's failure halts at any
.IR N ;
the devices in flight finish, the rest are recorded
.BR not_started_halt ,
and the exit is 102 or 103.
The gates apply between waves:
.B \-\-wave\-gate\-error\-count
and
.B \-\-wave\-gate\-error\-percent
stop a wave job when the wave just ended had that many failed devices, or
that share of its own, the rest recorded
.BR not_started_wave_gate ,
exit 104 or 105.
.B \-\-wave\-delay
pauses between waves, a
.I DURATION
.RB ( karvi (1)
VALUES) of at most
.BR 1h .
.B ssh.halt\-run\-on\-host\-key\-mismatch
stops new devices after the first changed host key, exit 114.
.PP
Each record names its device's mode, wave, width, and worker
.RB ( dispatch
in
.IR commands.jsonl ),
.I metrics.json
holds each wave's decision and the CPU signal behind it, and a dry run's
.B dispatch:
line names the mode and its widths.
.SH OUTPUT
In text, each device's output follows its header line, and the display
ends with the footer
.RB ( display.run.footer ,
.BR "! exit=EXIT elapsed=TIME artifacts=DIR" );
.B \-\-format jsonl
prints the records one per line and the job's summary as the last line;
.B \-\-format json
an indented array.
.PP
The job folder is
.IB output.root /YYMMDD/ JOB\-ID
(\fBoutput.root\fR \fBauto\fR: the shared jobs tree where it exists, else
.IR BASEDIR/jobs ).
It holds, each under an
.B output.files
key and all kept by default:
.TP
.I output.NAME.txt
each device's session as a terminal would show it, NAME the device name's
first label;
.TP
.I commands.jsonl
every record, one JSON object per line: the authority;
.TP
.IR errors.jsonl ", " failed\-devices.txt
the records that did not succeed, and the devices to run again;
.TP
.I commands.txt
the commands as requested;
.TP
.IR manifest.json ", " metrics.json ", " summary.json
the job as accepted, its measurements, and its result.
.PP
.B karvi run \-\-tf
.IB DIR /failed\-devices.txt
.B \-\-cf
.IB DIR /commands.txt
runs the failures again.
.B \-\-nof
keeps no folder (the footer says
.BR artifacts=none );
.BI \-\-of= PATH
writes it under
.IR PATH .
.BI \-\-cd= PATH
and
.BI \-\-fs= SUFFIX
also write each device's collection file, as
.BR karvi\-crun (1)
describes, unfiltered and with no hook.
.SH REHEARSAL
.B \-\-dry\-run
plans in the client and probes the daemon without starting one: it prints
the inspection report (each device's address, credential binding with no
value, transport, and port; the commands' count and digest; the dispatch
line) and submits nothing.
.B \-\-exercise
submits the plan and the credential package and has the daemon check
everything but the device: the report is printed and kept as
.I exercise.json
in the job folder, the summary reads
.BR exercised ,
and the exit is 0 when every target is ready, else the first finding's.
Neither sends a probe, opens a session, or runs a command; they exclude
each other, and neither combines with
.BR \-\-detach .
.PP
.B \-\-detach
prints the job ID and the folder once the daemon accepts the job and exits
0;
.B karvi job follow
.I JOB\-ID
renders it,
.B karvi watch
shows it.
.B \-\-follow=false
waits for the result without rendering.
Ctrl\-C during a run stops the follow alone: the job goes on in the
daemon, the client names it, and exits 113;
.B karvi job cancel
.I JOB\-ID
stops the job.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B karvi run \-\-site nyc \-\-cmd "show version" \-\-dp
Every device of the site, in a pool of the host's logical CPUs.
.TP
.B karvi run \-\-tf edge.txt \-\-cf change.txt \-\-dw \-\-halt\-on\-error\-percent 20
A wave job that starts no more devices once a fifth of those ended have
failed.
.TP
.B karvi run \-\-all \-\-cmd "show running\-config" \-\-fs=.cfg
Each device's configuration as
.I NAME.cfg
in the working directory, beside the job folder.
.TP
.B karvi run \-\-tl "r1 r2" \-\-cmd "show clock" \-\-dry\-run
What the run would do, contacting no device.
.TP
.B karvi run \-\-site nyc \-\-cmd "show clock" \-\-detach
Accepted and left to the daemon; follow it with
.BR "karvi job follow" .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR karvi (1),
.BR karvi\-crun (1),
.BR karvi\-job (1),
.BR karvi\-login (1),
.BR karvi\-watch (1),
